  "content": {
    "id": "p43-q55-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "55",
    "prompt": "A geometric series has 3rd term $12$ and 4th term $-3$. Find $a$, $r$ and the limiting sum.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$a=192$, $r=-\\frac14$, $S_\\infty=\\frac{768}{5}$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a_3&=12,\\qquad a_4=-3\\\\ar^2&=12\\qquad (1)\\\\ar^3&=-3\\qquad (2)\\end{align*}$$\nDividing (2) by (1):\n$$r=\\frac{-3}{12}=-\\frac14.$$\nSubstituting into (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}a\\left(\\frac1{16}\\right)&=12\\\\a&=192.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $|r|=\\frac14<1$, the limiting sum exists.\n$$\\begin{align*}S_\\infty&=\\frac{a}{1-r}\\\\&=\\frac{192}{1-\\left(-\\frac14\\right)}\\\\&=\\frac{768}{5}.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore $a=192$, $r=-\\frac14$ and $S_\\infty=\\frac{768}{5}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 53
  },
